  m_Name: ForwardCurvedCentrifugalFanData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Forward Curved Centrifugal Fan
  engineDescription: A Forward Curved Centrifugal Fan is a dynamic fan used to move
    air or gases by transferring rotational energy from a curved impeller to the
    flowing fluid. Its main components include the impeller, forward-curved blades,
    casing, shaft, motor, inlet, outlet, bearings, and supporting structure. During
    operation, air enters through the central inlet and is accelerated outward by
    the rotating impeller. The forward-curved blades provide a large number of blade
    passages that increase the velocity and flow rate of the air. The casing then
    collects the high-speed air and converts part of its kinetic energy into pressure
    before directing it through the outlet. Forward-curved centrifugal fans are widely
    used in HVAC systems, furnaces, air-handling equipment, ventilation systems,
    and low-pressure industrial applications. From an engineering perspective, understanding
    airflow, static pressure, blade geometry, fan curves, system resistance, efficiency,
    motor power, noise, vibration, and operating speed is essential for achieving
    effective and reliable air movement.
  engineCategory: Engineering
